The atomic memory instructions for wasm32.

The backend had no atomic patterns at all, so the middle end expanded every
__atomic builtin into a libcall -- `__atomic_load_4' and the rest of libatomic's
ABI.  libatomic has no wasm32 port and is configured off, so each of those was
an undefined symbol at the link.  That was invisible for as long as the target
had no thread model: libstdc++ uses __gnu_cxx::__atomic_add_single, which is
plain arithmetic, until a gthread model exists and it switches to the builtins.
So this is what --enable-threads=posix costs, and it has to land with it.

The instructions map onto the optabs closely enough that the patterns are
mechanical.  Three things are worth stating because the diff does not:

  - The memory-model operand is matched and ignored throughout.  wasm has
    exactly one ordering and it is sequential consistency -- there is no
    acquire, release or relaxed form of any of these instructions -- so a weaker
    model is served by being given a stronger guarantee than it asked for.  The
    same argument retires `is_weak' in the compare-exchange: a weak one may fail
    spuriously and this one never does.

  - `opname' already maps each rtl code to the string wasm spells it with, and
    -- checked against optabs.def -- to the string the optab is named with too,
    including ior -> "or".  So atomic_fetch_<opname> is both.  nand is left out
    because wasm has no rmw.nand and the middle end turns that one into a loop.

  - atomic_compare_and_swap is the one operation whose shape does not match the
    instruction: wasm's cmpxchg returns the value it found, while the optab wants
    that AND a boolean.  It is therefore an expand over an insn, with the boolean
    produced by emit_store_flag over the found and expected values -- which is
    the same answer a flag-setting target reports.  emit_store_flag rather than
    gen_cstore<mode>4 directly because cstore here is defined over SI and DI
    only, so a QI or HI compare-exchange needs its operands widened first.

  - **A compare-exchange's memory set must be opaque.** It writes memory only on
    success, so spelling the second set `(set mem desired)` is false for the failing
    case and lets a later load of the same address fold to the desired register. It is
    an unspec_volatile over mem, expected and desired instead -- the shape riscv's
    atomic_cas_value_strong and aarch64's @aarch64_compare_and_swap use, for this
    reason. The sibling atomic_exchange DOES spell its memory set plainly and is right
    to: an exchange always writes. The first cut of this patch had the plain form in
    both, which a review caught.

The features ASM_SPEC and the target_features section need are in 0015, with the
measurement that says the two patches cannot be separated: wasm-ld refuses
thread-local storage unless the object declares atomics and bulk-memory, so the
feature enablement is TLS's prerequisite as much as it is these instructions'.

Measured.  A GCC-compiled TU exercising fetch_add, load, store, exchange,
compare-exchange (succeeding and failing), a fence and a one-byte exchange links
and returns the right answer under node, with no __atomic_* libcall left in the
output.

--- a/gcc/config/wasm/attrs.md
+++ b/gcc/config/wasm/attrs.md
@@ -19,6 +19,12 @@
 ;; <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 
 (define_c_enum "unspecv" [
+  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_FENCE
+  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_LOAD
+  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_STORE
+  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_EXCHANGE
+  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_FETCH_OP
+  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_CMPXCHG
   UNSPECV_CALL_ADDRESS_BARRIER
 ])
 
@@ -49,6 +55,22 @@
   (QI "SI") (HI "SI")
   (SI "SI") (DI "DI")
   (SF "SF") (DF "DF")])
+;; How wasm names an atomic access of each width.  A sub-word atomic carries its
+;; width in the mnemonic and an unsigned-extension suffix (`rmw8.add_u'), a
+;; word-sized one carries neither (`rmw.add'), so one template covers both with
+;; two of these empty.  Every atomic is an i32 or i64 on the operand stack --
+;; there is no i8 value type -- which is why this is not `types'.
+(define_mode_attr atomic_type [(QI "i32") (HI "i32") (SI "i32") (DI "i64")])
+(define_mode_attr atomic_width [(QI "8") (HI "16") (SI "") (DI "")])
+;; Loads and read-modify-writes take the suffix; stores do not.
+(define_mode_attr atomic_u [(QI "_u") (HI "_u") (SI "") (DI "")])
+
+;; The read-modify-writes wasm has an instruction for.  `opname' already maps each
+;; of these to the string wasm spells it with, and -- checked against optabs.def --
+;; to the one the optab is named with too, including ior -> "or".  nand is absent
+;; because wasm has no rmw.nand; the middle end turns that one into a loop.
+(define_code_iterator atomicop [plus minus and ior xor])
+
 (define_mode_iterator SUBREGSI [QI HI])
 (define_mode_iterator SUBREGDI [QI HI SI])
 (define_mode_iterator P [(SI "Pmode == SImode") (DI "Pmode == DImode")])
--- a/gcc/config/wasm/wasm.md
+++ b/gcc/config/wasm/wasm.md
@@ -415,4 +415,136 @@
   [(set (subreg:REG (match_operand:<REG2F> 0 "wasm_register_operand") 0)
 	(match_operand:REG 1 "wasm_register_operand"))]
   ""
-  "%i1\n\t<reg2f_types>.reinterpret_<REG:types>\n\t%M0.set %0")
\ No newline at end of file
+  "%i1\n\t<reg2f_types>.reinterpret_<REG:types>\n\t%M0.set %0")
+;; Atomics
+;;
+;; These exist so that the middle end expands a __atomic builtin inline instead of
+;; into a libcall.  With no pattern it emits `__atomic_load_4' and the rest of
+;; libatomic's ABI, libatomic has no wasm32 port, and every one of them is then an
+;; undefined symbol at the link -- which is what threads would have cost, because
+;; libstdc++ drops __gnu_cxx::__atomic_add_single for these the moment a gthread
+;; model exists.
+;;
+;; The memory-model operand is matched and ignored throughout.  wasm has exactly
+;; one ordering and it is sequential consistency -- there is no acquire, release or
+;; relaxed form of any of these instructions -- so a weaker model is served by
+;; being given a stronger guarantee than it asked for, which is allowed.  The
+;; operand still has to be matched, because it is part of the optab's shape.
+;;
+;; `%i1' is the address and `%f1' its offset immediate, exactly as the non-atomic
+;; *load and *store patterns above spell them: an atomic instruction's memory
+;; argument has the same two parts as an ordinary one's.
+
+(define_insn "mem_thread_fence"
+  [(unspec_volatile [(match_operand:SI 0 "const_int_operand")]
+		    UNSPECV_ATOMIC_FENCE)]
+  ""
+  "atomic.fence")
+
+(define_insn "atomic_load<mode>"
+  [(set (match_operand:QHSDI 0 "register_operand")
+	(unspec_volatile:QHSDI
+	  [(match_operand:QHSDI 1 "memory_operand")
+	   (match_operand:SI 2 "const_int_operand")]
+	  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_LOAD))]
+  ""
+  "%i1\n\t<atomic_type>.atomic.load<atomic_width><atomic_u>\t%f1\n\t%o0")
+
+(define_insn "atomic_store<mode>"
+  [(set (match_operand:QHSDI 0 "memory_operand")
+	(unspec_volatile:QHSDI
+	  [(match_operand:QHSDI 1 "register_operand")
+	   (match_operand:SI 2 "const_int_operand")]
+	  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_STORE))]
+  ""
+  "%i0\n\t%i1\n\t<atomic_type>.atomic.store<atomic_width>\t%f0")
+
+;; Two sets, as the other targets spell these: the result is the value that was in
+;; memory, and memory separately becomes the new one.  One set over an
+;; unspec_volatile would emit the same instruction but would not say that the
+;; memory changed.
+(define_insn "atomic_exchange<mode>"
+  [(set (match_operand:QHSDI 0 "register_operand")
+	(unspec_volatile:QHSDI
+	  [(match_operand:QHSDI 1 "memory_operand")
+	   (match_operand:SI 3 "const_int_operand")]
+	  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_EXCHANGE))
+   (set (match_dup 1)
+	(match_operand:QHSDI 2 "register_operand"))]
+  ""
+  "%i1\n\t%i2\n\t<atomic_type>.atomic.rmw<atomic_width>.xchg<atomic_u>\t%f1\n\t%o0")
+
+(define_insn "atomic_fetch_<opname><mode>"
+  [(set (match_operand:QHSDI 0 "register_operand")
+	(match_operand:QHSDI 1 "memory_operand"))
+   (set (match_dup 1)
+	(unspec_volatile:QHSDI
+	  [(atomicop:QHSDI (match_dup 1)
+			   (match_operand:QHSDI 2 "register_operand"))
+	   (match_operand:SI 3 "const_int_operand")]
+	  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_FETCH_OP))]
+  ""
+  "%i1\n\t%i2\n\t<atomic_type>.atomic.rmw<atomic_width>.<opname><atomic_u>\t%f1\n\t%o0")
+
+;; The one operation whose shape does not match the instruction.  wasm's cmpxchg
+;; returns the value it found; the optab wants that AND a boolean saying whether
+;; the exchange happened.  So this is an expand: the instruction yields the old
+;; value, and the boolean is that value compared against the expected one, which
+;; is the same answer a flag-setting target would have reported.
+;;
+;; emit_store_flag rather than gen_cstore<mode>4 directly, for two reasons: it
+;; picks the comparison expansion the way every other caller does, and cstore is
+;; defined over SI and DI only, so a QI or HI compare-exchange needs its operands
+;; widened first -- which convert_to_mode below does and a direct gen_ could not.
+;;
+;; `is_weak' (operand 5) is ignored.  A weak compare-exchange may fail spuriously
+;; and this one never does, which is the stronger guarantee again.
+(define_expand "atomic_compare_and_swap<mode>"
+  [(match_operand:SI 0 "register_operand")
+   (match_operand:QHSDI 1 "register_operand")
+   (match_operand:QHSDI 2 "memory_operand")
+   (match_operand:QHSDI 3 "register_operand")
+   (match_operand:QHSDI 4 "register_operand")
+   (match_operand:SI 5 "const_int_operand")
+   (match_operand:SI 6 "const_int_operand")
+   (match_operand:SI 7 "const_int_operand")]
+  ""
+{
+  emit_insn (gen_atomic_cmpxchg<mode> (operands[1], operands[2], operands[3],
+				       operands[4], operands[6]));
+
+  rtx found = operands[1], expected = operands[3];
+  machine_mode cmp = <MODE>mode;
+  if (cmp != SImode && cmp != DImode)
+    {
+      found = convert_to_mode (SImode, found, 1);
+      expected = convert_to_mode (SImode, expected, 1);
+      cmp = SImode;
+    }
+  rtx flag = emit_store_flag (operands[0], EQ, found, expected, cmp, 1, 0);
+  gcc_assert (flag);
+  if (flag != operands[0])
+    emit_move_insn (operands[0], flag);
+  DONE;
+})
+
+(define_insn "atomic_cmpxchg<mode>"
+  [(set (match_operand:QHSDI 0 "register_operand")
+	(unspec_volatile:QHSDI
+	  [(match_operand:QHSDI 1 "memory_operand")
+	   (match_operand:QHSDI 2 "register_operand")
+	   (match_operand:QHSDI 3 "register_operand")
+	   (match_operand:SI 4 "const_int_operand")]
+	  UNSPECV_ATOMIC_CMPXCHG))
+   ;; Memory becomes an opaque function of all three, NOT unconditionally the
+   ;; desired value: a compare-exchange writes only on success, so
+   ;; `(set (match_dup 1) (match_dup 3))' is false for the failing case and lets a
+   ;; later load of the same address fold to the desired register.  The sibling
+   ;; atomic_exchange above spells its memory set plainly and is right to -- an
+   ;; exchange always writes.  Same shape as riscv's atomic_cas_value_strong and
+   ;; aarch64's @aarch64_compare_and_swap, for the same reason.
+   (set (match_dup 1)
+	(unspec_volatile:QHSDI [(match_dup 1) (match_dup 2) (match_dup 3)]
+			       UNSPECV_ATOMIC_CMPXCHG))]
+  ""
+  "%i1\n\t%i2\n\t%i3\n\t<atomic_type>.atomic.rmw<atomic_width>.cmpxchg<atomic_u>\t%f1\n\t%o0")
